            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">28. Textarea for Address</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;label for="address"&gt;
    Address:
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;textarea
    id="address"
    name="address"
    rows="4"
    cols="50"
    placeholder="Enter complete address"&gt;
&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">29. Textarea for Feedback</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;label for="feedback"&gt;
    Feedback:
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;textarea
    id="feedback"
    name="feedback"
    rows="6"
    maxlength="500"
    placeholder="Share your feedback"&gt;
&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">30. Textarea for Description</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;label for="description"&gt;
    Description:
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;textarea
    id="description"
    name="description"
    rows="8"
    minlength="20"
    maxlength="1000"
    required&gt;
&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">31. Textarea with Counter Concept</h2>

            <p>
                JavaScript can be used to display the current character count
                while the user types.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;textarea
    id="message"
    maxlength="200"
    rows="5"
    oninput="updateCount()"&gt;
&lt;/textarea&gt;

&lt;p id="counter"&gt;
    0 / 200
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;script&gt;

    function updateCount() {

        const message =
            document.getElementById("message");

        const counter =
            document.getElementById("counter");

        counter.textContent =
            message.value.length + " / 200";
    }

&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">32. Textarea with Disabled State</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;textarea
    rows="5"
    disabled&gt;
This field is unavailable.
&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">33. Textarea with Readonly State</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;textarea
    rows="5"
    readonly&gt;
This content can be read but not edited.
&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">34. Textarea with Required Validation</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;form&gt;

    &lt;label for="feedback"&gt;
        Feedback:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;textarea
        id="feedback"
        name="feedback"
        rows="5"
        required&gt;
    &lt;/textarea&gt;

    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;
        Submit
    &lt;/button&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">35. Multiple Textareas in One Form</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;form&gt;

    &lt;label for="address"&gt;
        Address:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;textarea
        id="address"
        name="address"
        rows="4"&gt;
    &lt;/textarea&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;label for="comments"&gt;
        Comments:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;textarea
        id="comments"
        name="comments"
        rows="5"&gt;
    &lt;/textarea&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">36. Textarea Accessibility</h2>

            <p>
                Textareas should have clear labels and appropriate instructions
                when necessary.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Use a meaningful label.</li>
                <li>Associate the label using <code>for</code> and <code>id</code>.</li>
                <li>Use suitable placeholder text as a supplementary hint.</li>
                <li>Use required and length constraints when appropriate.</li>
                <li>Provide clear validation messages.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">37. Common Mistakes</h2>

            <ul>
                <li>Forgetting the closing <code>&lt;/textarea&gt;</code> tag.</li>
                <li>Using a <code>value</code> attribute to set initial textarea content.</li>
                <li>Forgetting the <code>name</code> attribute.</li>
                <li>Using placeholder text as the only label.</li>
                <li>Using excessive fixed width that breaks responsive layouts.</li>
                <li>Applying unnecessary restrictions to users.</li>
                <li>Forgetting server-side validation.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">38. Best Practices</h2>

            <ul>
                <li>Use textarea for genuinely multi-line input.</li>
                <li>Use clear labels.</li>
                <li>Set a reasonable initial size.</li>
                <li>Use responsive CSS for width.</li>
                <li>Use <code>maxlength</code> and <code>minlength</code> when appropriate.</li>
                <li>Allow useful resizing when it improves usability.</li>
                <li>Validate submitted content on the server.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">39. Complete Contact Form Example</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;

&lt;head&gt;

    &lt;title&gt;Contact Form&lt;/title&gt;

    &lt;style&gt;

        .form-group {
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }

        .form-group label {
            display: block;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 6px;
        }

        textarea {
            width: 100%;
            max-width: 700px;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            resize: vertical;
            padding: 10px;
        }

    &lt;/style&gt;

&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;

    &lt;h1&gt;Contact Us&lt;/h1&gt;

    &lt;form action="/contact"
          method="post"&gt;

        &lt;div class="form-group"&gt;

            &lt;label for="name"&gt;
                Full Name:
            &lt;/label&gt;

            &lt;input type="text"
                   id="name"
                   name="name"
                   required&gt;

        &lt;/div&gt;

        &lt;div class="form-group"&gt;

            &lt;label for="email"&gt;
                Email:
            &lt;/label&gt;

            &lt;input type="email"
                   id="email"
                   name="email"
                   required&gt;

        &lt;/div&gt;

        &lt;div class="form-group"&gt;

            &lt;label for="message"&gt;
                Message:
            &lt;/label&gt;

            &lt;textarea
                id="message"
                name="message"
                rows="6"
                maxlength="1000"
                placeholder="Enter your message"
                required&gt;
            &lt;/textarea&gt;

        &lt;/div&gt;

        &lt;button type="submit"&gt;
            Send Message
        &lt;/button&gt;

    &lt;/form&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;

&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">40. Practice Task</h2>

            <div class="example-box">
                <p><strong>Create a form containing:</strong></p>

                <ol>
                    <li>A message textarea with a label.</li>
                    <li>An address textarea.</li>
                    <li>A feedback textarea.</li>
                    <li>A required textarea.</li>
                    <li>A textarea with <code>maxlength</code>.</li>
                    <li>A readonly textarea.</li>
                    <li>A disabled textarea.</li>
                    <li>A responsive textarea using CSS.</li>
                    <li>A textarea with a character counter using JavaScript.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
                <h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    The HTML <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> element is used for
                    multi-line text input. Important attributes include
                    <code>rows</code>, <code>cols</code>, <code>name</code>,
                    <code>id</code>, <code>placeholder</code>, <code>required</code>,
                    <code>maxlength</code>, <code>minlength</code>,
                    <code>readonly</code>, <code>disabled</code>,
                    <code>autocomplete</code>, and <code>wrap</code>.
                    CSS can be used to create responsive layouts and control
                    resizing and visual appearance.
                </p>
